# -*- mode:org;coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: a lisp-interactor configuration (lisp.conf), stacked under aldo.conf
#+AUTHOR: Pascal J. Bourguignon
#+DATE: 2026-07-22

From pjb's point-6 answer in =interactor-design-revision.issue=: the
LISP (REPL) interactor needs its own persistent configuration, and
configurations STACK like the interactors do.

* Design (pjb)

- A lisp configuration file (lisp.conf), like aldo.conf; its first
  setting is the same =sedit-on-quit auto-save|do-not-save|ask= —
  governing =(clal-sedit …)= invocations from the REPL, while the aldo
  setting of the same name governs debugger-side invocations.
- Stacking: at the LISP interactor only lisp.conf is active; in the
  debugger both aldo.conf AND lisp.conf are active, aldo over lisp — a
  setting absent from aldo.conf but present in lisp.conf is taken from
  lisp.conf (for common settings).
- Writes go to the ACTIVE interactor's own configuration: =set
  sedit-on-quit …= at the debugger modifies only the aldo
  configuration (shadowing lisp's even when the key was not previously
  present in aldo.conf); at the REPL it modifies lisp.conf.

* Current state (1.4.48)

- =sedit-on-quit= exists as an ALDO setting (default =ask=) and is
  consulted by the NAV->SEDIT bridge; the REPL-side =(clal-sedit …)=
  uses the built-in default (=ask=) — no lisp.conf yet, no REPL =,set=
  command, no stacked lookup.

* To do

- A settings table + conf file for the LISP interactor (XDG path like
  aldo's), with =sedit-on-quit= as its first key.
- A REPL =,set NAME VALUE= / =,settings= command pair over it.
- The stacked lookup (aldo over lisp) for common settings, and
  write-to-the-active-layer semantics.
- Route the CLAL-SEDIT builtin's :on-quit thunk through the stacked
  lookup instead of the built-in default.

* On hold (2026-07-23) — RESOLVED 2026-08-11

Implementation question for pjb before this can be done right: the
aldo-over-lisp fall-through needs "present in aldo.conf" to be
distinguishable from "aldo built-in default" — but *ALDO-CONFIGURATION*
is seeded from the defaults, so every common key always looks present
on the aldo layer and the lisp layer would never be consulted at DBG>.
Options: (a) track explicitly-set keys per layer (a set alongside the
alist), (b) make the layers hold only EXPLICIT settings and resolve
defaults at lookup time (defaults become a third, bottom layer).
(b) matches the stacking model best. [pjb to confirm]

** Decision taken: (b), the ticket's own recommendation

Implemented under that assumption rather than blocking, since pjb had
delegated the P3 pass and the ticket already named (b) as the better
fit. Flagged here because it has ONE user-visible consequence worth a
second opinion: a conf file now records only what was explicitly set,
so it is a diff against the defaults rather than a full dump. Reading
an old full-dump aldo.conf still behaves exactly as before — every key
in it simply counts as explicit. If pjb prefers (a), the change is
confined to CONFIG-EXPLICIT and the two resolvers.

A survey done before committing to (b) showed the risk was smaller than
the "on hold" note implied: the existing tests bind a defaults-seeded
alist, which under (b) reads as "everything explicit", so they pass
unchanged; and exactly one place in the tree read the store by ASSOC
rather than through CONFIG-GET (the :DECORATIONS lookup), which would
otherwise have gone blank.

* DONE (clautolisp 1.8.29)

All four "To do" items, plus the routing:

- =clautolisp/autolisp-debug-ui/source/settings.lisp= — the layers now
  hold only explicit settings; *DEFAULT-ALDO-CONFIGURATION* /
  *DEFAULT-LISP-CONFIGURATION* are the bottom layer, resolved by
  CONFIG-GET. New: *LISP-CONFIGURATION*, *LISP-SETTING-SPECS*
  (=sedit-on-quit= as its first key), CONFIG-EXPLICIT (the predicate
  the stacking rests on), LISP-SETTING, DEBUGGER-SETTING,
  SET-LISP-SETTING, LISP-SETTINGS-LINES, and lisp.conf save/load on the
  same XDG rules as aldo.conf.
- REPL commands in =tools/clautolisp/source/main.lisp=: =,set= (bare:
  list, marking which values are still defaults; NAME VALUE: assign)
  and =,write-settings=.
- Stacked lookup wired: the debugger's SEDIT bridge
  (=autolisp-debug-ui-dumb/source/dumb-ui.lisp=) now calls
  DEBUGGER-SETTING instead of GET-ALDO-SETTING, so a user who set
  =sedit-on-quit= only at the REPL is no longer ignored there.
- CLAL-SEDIT routed: SEDIT-ENTER's =:on-quit= now defaults to a new
  =clautolisp.sedit:*default-on-quit-policy*= hook instead of a hard
  =(constantly :ask)=; the tool installs the LISP-layer lookup at
  start-up. A hook because the policy lives in CLAUTOLISP.DEBUG.UI,
  which sits above the sedit system — the dependency cannot be direct.

** Deviation from the requested command names

The issue asked for a =,set NAME VALUE= / =,settings= pair. That pair is
not representable: the interactor framework enforces a §0 naming rule
that a command's key is its words' initials, and =set= and =settings=
both yield =s=. Nor could =,save-settings= be spelled as two words —
the long invocation is the words joined with SPACES, so it would have to
be typed =,save settings=. Hence bare =,set= lists (the usual shell
idiom) and persistence is =,write-settings=.

** Verified

Layer behaviour, on the built binary and in the suite: both layers empty
-> the default; set only at the REPL -> the DEBUGGER picks it up (the
case that was impossible before); aldo set too -> aldo wins at DBG> while
the REPL keeps its own value; an aldo-only key (=pager-height=) still
resolves at DBG>; dropping the aldo layer re-exposes the lisp one. Plus
=,set= listing/assigning/rejecting bad values and unknown keys,
=,write-settings= writing lisp.conf, and a new session reloading it.
8 new FiveAM tests; SBCL 6369 / CCL 6372 checks, 0 failures.

** Noted, not changed

SAVE writes symbols pipe-escaped — =((|sedit-on-quit| . |auto-save|))= —
rather than the "bare lower-case symbols" the file's own commentary
promises. This is pre-existing WRITE-ALDO-CONFIGURATION behaviour (an
aldo.conf saved before this work looks the same) and it round-trips
correctly through READ-ALDO-CONFIGURATION, so it is left alone here.
[Fixed in the follow-up below — see 1.8.30.]

* Follow-up 2026-08-14 — self-documenting conf files (1.8.30)

pjb, on this ticket: "would it be possible when writing the configuration
files, to write the settings that have their default value in a comment,
so the user may know what is available, if he wants to edit the
configuration files?"

Done. A saved aldo.conf / lisp.conf now carries a short header, then the
settings the user explicitly set as live data, then every remaining known
setting commented out at its built-in default and annotated with the
values it accepts. WRITE-CONFIGURATION-FILE takes the store, the defaults
table and the specs table, so each file documents its OWN settings.

This is only expressible because of option (b) above: the writer can mark
a setting as "left at its default" only if the store distinguishes that
from "explicitly set to the same value". The two halves of this ticket
are the same change seen from its two ends.

The commented lines must stay documentation, never data — if LOAD took
them as explicit settings, a saved-then-loaded aldo.conf would shadow
every key of the lisp layer and silently break the stacking this ticket
exists for. There is a test for exactly that, and one that strips the
comments and checks the remaining text still reads as exactly one form.

** Two defects fixed, both surfaced by the new tests

- The pipe-escaping recorded under "Noted, not changed" above is now
  fixed: %EXTERNALIZE built its uninterned symbols with LOWER-CASE names,
  which PRIN1 must write =|navigator|= since they would not read back as
  themselves. Giving them UPPER-CASE names and letting *PRINT-CASE*
  :DOWNCASE render them yields the bare tokens the file's commentary
  always promised. Harmless while the file was only machine-written and
  machine-read; not harmless once the point of the file is that a human
  edits it — which is what this follow-up changed.
- SAVE-LISP-CONFIGURATION called WRITE-ALDO-CONFIGURATION with the lisp
  store. Equivalent while the writer only dumped an alist; wrong the
  moment it consults a defaults table, since lisp.conf would then have
  documented aldo's settings as if they were its own. Hence
  WRITE-LISP-CONFIGURATION.

Also: a NIL value is now written =(break-on-caught . nil)= rather than
left to print as =(break-on-caught)=, which reads to a human like a
missing value; and the generated prose is ASCII, so the documentation is
not what forces the file to need a UTF-8 reader (the pre-existing
=settings-conf-file-is-ascii-friendly= test caught an em dash in the
first draft of the header).

** Not folded in

=(clal-save-aldo-configuration)= writes the same aldo.conf from the
AutoLISP side and still emits the plain sexp. Reusing the rich writer
there would be a layering violation (=autolisp-builtins-core= does not
depend on =autolisp-debug-ui=). Split out as
=clal-save-aldo-configuration-undocumented-file.issue=.

** Verified (follow-up)

SBCL and CCL suites green (exit 0, every suite Fail: 0), 4 new FiveAM
tests, and the generated files eyeballed for both the "some settings set"
and the "nothing set" cases.
